<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albania</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Albania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albania, officially the Republic of Albania, is a country in Southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> on the Adriatic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Seas</a>. Once home to Illyrian tribes, it passed to the Roman and Byzantine empires, and in the 15th century resisted Ottoman expansion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Gjergj Kastrioti</a> Skanderbeg for over two decades before being incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. It declared independence in 1912. The twentieth century brought monarchy, foreign occupation in both World Wars, and a communist regime under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>, before a shift to an open market economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albania.jpg" alt="Albania" width="330" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sadik Kaceli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Albania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory was inhabited by several Illyrian tribes, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardiaei">Ardiaei</a>, Bylliones, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dassaretae">Dassaretii</a>, Enchele and Taulantians, while the Epirote Chaonians, whose capital was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenice">Phoenice</a>, lived in the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> founded colonies on the coast by the 7th century BCE, notably Apollonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Epidamnos</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> were mentioned in the records for the last time in the 7th century CE, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and absorbed much of the Balkan population.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> divided in 395, on the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, and the region remained with the Eastern empire. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> several Albanian principalities arose, among them the Principality of Arbanon, the Kingdom of Albania, the Principality of Albania and Albania Veneta. Gjon Buzuku's Meshari, a Catholic missal published in 1555, is counted among the earliest literary works in written Albanian, and some evidence suggests the language was written from at least the 14th century.</p><p>An Albanian Renaissance, rooted in the late 18th century, developed national culture and literature during the 19th. Independence was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a> in 1912 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-qemali">Ismail Qemali</a>, who raised the double-headed eagle flag. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the country had no settled government or recognised borders, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> each sought influence; the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> for protection in 1918 and was refused.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>'s People's Socialist Republic followed Marxist–Leninist ideas. It prohibited religious practice, restricted travel severely and abolished private property, and its rule was marked by purges and repression. Opposition met internal exile, long imprisonment or execution. Foreign tourism was tightly controlled, and the ban on American visitors was lifted in 1990 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiz-alia">Ramiz Alia</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">revolutions of 1989</a>, student activism grew. The first multi-party elections were held in 1991, and the Democratic Party defeated the communists in the parliamentary elections of 1992. Albania joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 2009, applied for membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> on 28 April 2009 and was granted candidate status on 24 June 2014.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Albania covers 28,748 square kilometres between the Adriatic and Ionian seas and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Mount Korab, at 2,764 metres, is the highest point. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a> is the capital and largest city. The 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 2,402,113 inhabitants. The official language is Albanian, standardised from the Gheg and Tosk dialects, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkumbin">Shkumbin</a> river as their rough boundary. The country is a parliamentary republic with no official religion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 135 another Alan invasion struck <b>Albania</b>, Media and Armenia; Flavius Arrian, governor of Cappadocia, prepared his army to meet them and wrote a short treatise, the Order of Battle against the Alans, describing how Roman infantry should receive the charge of heavy cavalry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The land between the Caspian Sea and the Caucasus was ruled first by Caucasian <b>Albania</b> and then by a succession of Persian empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Caucasian <b>Albania</b>, known in Armenian as Aghvank and later in Arabic as Arran, lay in the eastern Caucasus between the Greater Caucasus range, the Caspian Sea and the Kura river, roughly in the north and centre of modern Azerbaijan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Greece has the largest economy in the Balkans and is an important regional investor: the second foreign investor in <b>Albania</b> and the largest in North Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After annexing <b>Albania</b> in 1939, Italy at its height in the early Second World War also occupied territory in Greece, Yugoslavia and south-eastern France, but its armed forces struggled against better-equipped Allied armies, particularly in North Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…vassal of Armenia in Artaxias' time, and at the height of Tigranes' rule Parthia itself and some Arab tribes were his vassals; the kingdom was bordered by Caucasian <b>Albania</b> to the east, Iberia to the north along the Kur, Rome to the west and Parthia to the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
